IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `qwhs-dates`
IMPORT `qwhs-rules`

-- Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) -- the questions of the WORKER.
--
-- SEAT: the worker (or prospective worker) to whom the Act is applied when they are hurt or put at risk at work, cease
-- unsafe work, are treated badly for raising safety, want someone prosecuted, or are affected by an inspector's
-- decision: "Am I a worker?", "Was my injury a serious injury?", "Must my employer notify the regulator?", "May I stop
-- this work?", "Can I take my employer to the commission for what they did to me, and by when?", "What orders can I
-- get?", "Can I ask for a prosecution, and by when must I be answered?", "Can I have this decision reviewed, and by
-- when?". The same questions serve a family member of a worker who died where the Act lets "a person" act (s 231). The
-- business, its officers, the regulator, the inspector, the WHS prosecutor, the commission and the court get no seat:
-- their duties appear as what the worker is owed and by when, their judgments as inputs.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, numbers, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of qwhs-rules.l4.
-- The time limits are DEONTIC rules run inside the "on this day" questions; no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (c) The State of Queensland,
-- CC BY 4.0; not the authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See
-- NOTES.md.

§ `Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld): the worker's questions`

§§ `Am I covered`

@export Am I a "worker" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 5, 7? You are if you carry out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or undertaking (for profit or not) -- as an employee, contractor or subcontractor or their employee, labour hire worker, outworker, apprentice or trainee, work experience student or volunteer -- and police officers are workers. A group made up only of volunteers, none of whom employs anyone, does not conduct a business or undertaking, so its volunteers are not workers of it.
GIVEN `do I carry out work for them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you carry out work, in any capacity (paid or unpaid), for the person or organisation
      `do they conduct a business or undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they conduct a business or undertaking (a trade, business, government or other undertaking, alone or with others, for profit or not)
      `are they a volunteer association` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they are a group of volunteers working together for community purposes, none of whom employs anyone to carry out work for the group
      `does a regulation say they are not one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation under s 5(6) says they are taken not to conduct a business or undertaking (answer no if unsure)
      `am I a police officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a Queensland police officer
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a worker under the Act` MEANS
    `s 7 -- I am a worker` `do I carry out work for them` (`s 5 -- it is a person conducting a business or undertaking` `do they conduct a business or undertaking` `are they a volunteer association` `does a regulation say they are not one`) `am I a police officer`

§§ `What happened to me at work`
-- Situation: an incident at work. Ask "Has someone been hurt, or nearly hurt, at work?" before this group.

@export Was my injury or illness a "serious injury or illness" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 36? It is if it needed immediate treatment as a hospital in-patient; immediate treatment for an amputation, serious head or eye injury, serious burn, degloving or scalping, spinal injury, loss of a bodily function or serious lacerations; or medical treatment within 48 hours of exposure to a substance -- plus anything a regulation adds, minus anything a regulation excludes.
GIVEN `did I need immediate in-patient treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury or illness required immediate treatment as an in-patient in a hospital
      `what immediate treatment did I need` IS AN `Immediate treatment` @desc Which of the listed injuries the immediate treatment was for, or none of these
      `did I need treatment within 48 hours of exposure to a substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it required medical treatment within 48 hours of exposure to a substance
      `does a regulation include it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation prescribes this kind of injury or illness as a serious injury or illness (answer no if unsure)
      `does a regulation exclude it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation prescribes this kind of injury or illness as not included (answer no if unsure)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was my injury or illness a serious injury or illness` MEANS
    `s 36 -- serious injury or illness` `did I need immediate in-patient treatment` `what immediate treatment did I need` `did I need treatment within 48 hours of exposure to a substance` `does a regulation include it` `does a regulation exclude it`

@export Was what happened a "dangerous incident" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 37? It is an incident in relation to a workplace that exposed a worker or anyone else to a serious risk to health or safety from an immediate or imminent exposure to one of the listed events (an uncontrolled escape of a substance, gas, steam or pressurised substance; an uncontrolled explosion or fire; electric shock; something falling from a height; collapse or failure of authorised plant, a structure or an excavation; an inrush or loss of ventilation underground; or a prescribed event), unless a regulation excludes it.
GIVEN `did it happen in relation to a workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident happened in relation to a workplace (a place where work is carried out for a business or undertaking, including vehicles and vessels)
      `was there a serious risk to someone's health or safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it exposed a worker or another person to a serious risk to health or safety
      `what was the exposure to` IS AN `Exposure` @desc What the immediate or imminent exposure was to, from the list in s 37, or none of these
      `does a regulation exclude it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation prescribes this kind of incident as not a dangerous incident (answer no if unsure)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was what happened a dangerous incident` MEANS
    `s 37 -- dangerous incident` `did it happen in relation to a workplace` `was there a serious risk to someone's health or safety` `what was the exposure to` `does a regulation exclude it`

@export Is what happened a "notifiable incident" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 35? It is the death of a person, a serious injury or illness of a person, or a dangerous incident. Answer the serious injury and dangerous incident questions first.
GIVEN `did someone die` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person died
      `was it a serious injury or illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a serious injury or illness (see the serious injury question)
      `was it a dangerous incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a dangerous incident (see the dangerous incident question)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is what happened a notifiable incident` MEANS
    `s 35 -- notifiable incident` `did someone die` (`yes or no` `was it a serious injury or illness`) (`yes or no` `was it a dangerous incident`)

@export Must the business notify the regulator (Workplace Health and Safety Queensland) of what happened to me (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 38)? The person conducting the business or undertaking must ensure the regulator is notified immediately after becoming aware that a notifiable incident arising out of the conduct of the business has occurred, by telephone or in writing and by the fastest possible means (maximum penalty 100 penalty units).
GIVEN `is it a notifiable incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a notifiable incident (see the notifiable incident question)
      `did it arise out of the conduct of the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident arose out of the conduct of the business or undertaking
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the business notify the regulator of my incident` MEANS
    `s 38(1) -- the business must notify the regulator` (`yes or no` `is it a notifiable incident`) `did it arise out of the conduct of the business`

@export Until when must the business keep its record of my notifiable incident (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 38(7))? For at least 5 years from the day notice of the incident was given to the regulator (maximum penalty 50 penalty units).
GIVEN `the day notice was given to the regulator` IS A DATE @desc The day the business gave notice of the incident to the regulator
GIVETH A DATE
`Until when must the business keep the record of my incident` MEANS
    `s 38(7) -- the record must be kept until` `the day notice was given to the regulator`

@export Does the duty to leave the site of my incident undisturbed stop this action (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 39)? The person managing or controlling the workplace must ensure, so far as reasonably practicable, that the site (with any plant, substance, structure or thing involved) is not disturbed until an inspector arrives or an earlier time an inspector directs; but that does not prevent helping an injured person, removing a person who has died, making the site safe, a police investigation, or what an inspector or the regulator permits.
GIVEN `is it a notifiable incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a notifiable incident (see the notifiable incident question)
      `has an inspector arrived or released the site` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inspector has arrived at the site, or the earlier time an inspector directed has come
      `what is the action` IS AN `Action at the site` @desc What the action that would disturb the site is for
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the duty to leave the site undisturbed stop this action` MEANS
    `s 39 -- the site must not be disturbed by the action` (`yes or no` `is it a notifiable incident`) `has an inspector arrived or released the site` `what is the action`

§§ `Stopping unsafe work`
-- Situation: the worker faces work they think is unsafe. Ask "Are you being asked to do work you think is unsafe?" first.

@export May I stop, or refuse to do, this work because it is unsafe (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 84)? A worker may cease or refuse to carry out work if they have a reasonable concern that doing it would expose them to a serious risk to their health or safety from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard. Whether a concern is reasonable is for the commission or a court to judge; the answer is conditional on it.
GIVEN `am I a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker of the business (see the worker question)
      `am I concerned the work would expose me to a risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are concerned that doing the work would expose you to a risk to your health or safety
      `is my concern reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your concern is a reasonable one (a judgment: answer as you expect a tribunal would find)
      `is the risk to me serious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk is a serious risk to your own health or safety
      `is it from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk comes from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard (not a long-term or distant one)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I stop or refuse this unsafe work` MEANS
    `s 84 -- I may cease or refuse to carry out the work` (`yes or no` `am I a worker`) `am I concerned the work would expose me to a risk` `is my concern reasonable` `is the risk to me serious` `is it from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard`

@export Must I tell the business that I have stopped work (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 86(a))? Yes, as soon as practicable, if you stopped on your own concern under s 84; not if you stopped on a direction from your health and safety representative (who must give the business the cease work notice).
GIVEN `how did I stop work` IS A `How I ceased work` @desc Whether you stopped on your own concern (s 84), on a health and safety representative's direction (s 85), or have not stopped work under these sections
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I tell the business that I have stopped work` MEANS
    `s 86(a) -- I must notify the business that I have ceased work` `how did I stop work`

@export Must I stay available for other work after stopping unsafe work (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 86(b))? Yes: a worker who ceases work under the division must remain available to carry out suitable alternative work.
GIVEN `how did I stop work` IS A `How I ceased work` @desc Whether you stopped on your own concern (s 84), on a health and safety representative's direction (s 85), or have not stopped work under these sections
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I stay available for suitable alternative work` MEANS
    `s 86(b) -- I must remain available for suitable alternative work` `how did I stop work`

@export Can the business direct me to do other work while I have stopped unsafe work (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 87)? Yes, suitable alternative work at the same or another workplace, if that work is safe and appropriate for you, until you can resume your normal duties.
GIVEN `how did I stop work` IS A `How I ceased work` @desc Whether you stopped on your own concern (s 84), on a health and safety representative's direction (s 85), or have not stopped work under these sections
      `is the other work suitable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other work is suitable alternative work
      `is it safe and appropriate for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other work is safe and appropriate for you to carry out
      `can I resume normal duties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can now resume your normal duties
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the business direct me to other work` MEANS
    `s 87 -- the business may direct me to alternative work` `how did I stop work` `is the other work suitable` `is it safe and appropriate for me` `can I resume normal duties`

@export Is the continuity of my engagement protected after I stopped unsafe work (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 88)? Stopping work under the division does not affect the continuity of your engagement for the purposes a regulation prescribes, unless you unreasonably failed to follow a direction to do suitable alternative work that was safe and appropriate for you. If you did, the Act no longer protects it but does not say it is broken.
GIVEN `how did I stop work` IS A `How I ceased work` @desc Whether you stopped on your own concern (s 84), on a health and safety representative's direction (s 85), or have not stopped work under these sections
      `was I directed to other work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business directed you to carry out alternative work
      `was that work suitable, safe and appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that work was suitable, and safe and appropriate for you, at the same or another workplace
      `did I unreasonably fail to do it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you unreasonably failed to comply with that direction (a judgment)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my continuity of engagement protected` MEANS
    `s 88 -- my continuity of engagement is protected` `how did I stop work` `was I directed to other work` `was that work suitable, safe and appropriate` `did I unreasonably fail to do it`

§§ `Treated badly for raising safety`
-- Situation: the worker was dismissed, disadvantaged, threatened, coerced or misled over a safety matter. Ask "Were you
-- treated badly because of a work health and safety matter?" first.

@export Was what was done to me "discriminatory conduct" for a "prohibited reason" (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 105, 106)? Discriminatory conduct is dismissing you, ending your contract, putting you to a detriment, altering your position to your detriment or treating you less favourably than other workers; not engaging you, or offering you worse terms, as a prospective worker; or ending or refusing a commercial arrangement -- or organising or threatening any of these. It is for a prohibited reason if done because you hold or held a safety role, use or used powers or functions under the Act, helped or informed an inspector, raised a safety issue or concern, helped resolve one, or sought compliance with the Act (including proposing to).
GIVEN `what was done to me` IS A `What was done` @desc What was done, organised or threatened against you
      `why was it done` IS A `Reason` @desc The reason it was done, from the list in s 106, or another reason
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was it discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason` MEANS
    IF `s 105 -- discriminatory conduct` `what was done to me` EQUALS JUST TRUE AND `s 106 -- a prohibited reason` `why was it done` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

@export Was the action taken or threatened against me prohibited coercion or inducement (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 108)? A person must not take, organise or threaten action (including not taking an action) against you with intent to coerce or induce you or anyone to use or not use a power or function under the Act, to use it in a particular way, or to give up or not seek a role under the Act (maximum penalty 1,000 penalty units). A reasonable direction by an emergency services worker in an emergency is not coercion.
GIVEN `was action taken or threatened against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone took, organised or threatened action against you (or refused or threatened to refuse to take an action)
      `was it meant to coerce or induce` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did it with intent to coerce or induce you or another person
      `what was it meant to achieve` IS AN `Aim of the action` @desc What they meant to make you (or someone) do
      `was it a reasonable emergency direction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a reasonable direction given by an emergency services worker in an emergency
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was it prohibited coercion or inducement` MEANS
    `s 108 -- prohibited coercion or inducement` `was action taken or threatened against me` `was it meant to coerce or induce` `what was it meant to achieve` `was it a reasonable emergency direction`

@export Was what I was told a prohibited misrepresentation (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 109)? A person must not knowingly or recklessly make a false or misleading representation to you about your rights or obligations under the Act, your ability to start or take part in a process or proceedings under it, or your ability to complain or inquire to someone who enforces it (maximum penalty 1,000 penalty units) -- unless you would not be expected to rely on it. This is an offence only: the commission cannot make orders about it under s 112.
GIVEN `was it false or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representation was false or misleading
      `did they know, or were they reckless` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they made it knowing it was false or misleading, or recklessly
      `what was it about` IS A `Subject of the representation` @desc What the representation was about
      `would I be expected to rely on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would be expected to rely on it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was it a prohibited misrepresentation` MEANS
    `s 109 -- prohibited misrepresentation` `was it false or misleading` `did they know, or were they reckless` `what was it about` `would I be expected to rely on it`

@export Can I (or someone for me) apply to the Queensland Industrial Relations Commission for orders about how I was treated (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 112(1), (6), 115)? A person affected by discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason, by someone procuring it, or by coercion under s 108 may apply; so may a suitable entity (such as a union) representing a worker affected as a worker, or a representative authorised by a person affected other than as a worker. Not while another proceeding, application or complaint on the same matter under another Commonwealth or State law is on foot, and not if you failed in one (other than for workers' compensation).
GIVEN `what conduct is it about` IS A `Conduct complained of` @desc Which kind of conduct the application is about
      `who would apply` IS A `Who is applying` @desc Who would make the application
      `was I affected as a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were affected as a worker (rather than, say, as a business in a commercial arrangement)
      `is another proceeding of mine on the same matter on foot` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have started a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under another Commonwealth or State law and not withdrawn it
      `have I failed in another proceeding on the same matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed in a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under another Commonwealth or State law
      `was that proceeding about workers' compensation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that failed proceeding related to workers' compensation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to the commission for orders` MEANS
    IF `s 112(1), (6) -- the applicant may apply to the commission` `what conduct is it about` `who would apply` `was I affected as a worker` EQUALS JUST TRUE
       AND `s 115(a), (c) -- I am barred from applying` `the other proceedings` EQUALS JUST FALSE
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `the other proceedings` MEANS
            `Other proceedings` WITH
                `a court made a s 111 order for the same conduct`        IS FALSE
                `another proceeding of mine on the same matter is pending` IS `is another proceeding of mine on the same matter on foot`
                `I received compensation for the matter under another law` IS FALSE
                `I failed in another proceeding on the same matter`        IS `have I failed in another proceeding on the same matter`
                `that proceeding was about workers' compensation`          IS `was that proceeding about workers' compensation`
                -- the first and third fields are read only by the orders questions: fixed placeholders here.

@export By when must I start my application to the Industrial Relations Commission about discriminatory or coercive conduct (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 113(1))? Not more than 1 year after the day you knew, or ought to have known, that the cause of action accrued.
GIVEN `the day I knew or ought to have known` IS A DATE @desc The day you knew, or ought to have known, that you had a cause of action (usually the day of the conduct, or the day you learned of it)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply to the commission` MEANS
    `s 113(1) -- the last day to commence` `the day I knew or ought to have known`

@export Can I still start my application to the Industrial Relations Commission on this day (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 113(1))? Yes up to and including the day 1 year after the day you knew or ought to have known of the cause of action; the Act gives the commission no power to extend it.
GIVEN `the day I knew or ought to have known` IS A DATE @desc The day you knew, or ought to have known, that you had a cause of action
      `the day I would apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you would file the application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I still apply to the commission on this day` MEANS
    `yes or no` (`commence the proceeding` `by` `the applicant` `on` `the day I would apply` `is in time for` (`s 113(1) -- the last day to commence` `the day I knew or ought to have known`))

@export Can the commission find that the conduct against me was for a prohibited reason (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 112(4), 113(2))? Only if a prohibited reason was a substantial reason for it; but once you allege a prohibited reason, it is presumed to be a substantial reason unless the other side proves on the balance of probabilities that it was not.
GIVEN `what was done to me` IS A `What was done` @desc What was done, organised or threatened against you
      `the reason I allege` IS A `Reason` @desc The prohibited reason you allege, from the list in s 106
      `have they proved it was not a substantial reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other side has proved, on the balance of probabilities, that the reason was not a substantial reason (a finding for the commission)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the commission find a prohibited reason` MEANS
    `ss 112(4), 113(2) -- discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason can be found` (`s 105 -- discriminatory conduct` `what was done to me`) (`s 106 -- a prohibited reason` `the reason I allege`) `have they proved it was not a substantial reason`

@export Does the person I am claiming against have the s 113(3) defence (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld))? For discriminatory conduct, or procuring it, it is a defence if they prove both that the conduct was reasonable in the circumstances and that a substantial reason for it was to comply with the Act or a corresponding WHS law. The section gives no defence to coercion under s 108.
GIVEN `what conduct is it about` IS A `Conduct complained of` @desc Which kind of conduct the application is about
      `have they proved it was reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they have proved the conduct was reasonable in the circumstances (a finding for the commission)
      `have they proved a substantial reason was to comply with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they have proved a substantial reason for the conduct was to comply with the Act or a corresponding WHS law (a finding for the commission)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do they have the s 113(3) defence` MEANS
    `s 113(3) -- the other side has a defence` `what conduct is it about` `have they proved it was reasonable` `have they proved a substantial reason was to comply with the Act`

@export What orders can the Industrial Relations Commission make for me (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 112(3), 114(3), 115(b))? For discriminatory conduct: an injunction, compensation paid to you, reinstatement or re-employment (or employment in the position you applied for) if you were or are an employee or prospective employee, and any other appropriate order. For procuring it: an injunction, compensation and other orders. For coercion: an injunction and other orders, but no compensation. None if a court already made a s 111 order for the same conduct; no compensation if you received compensation for the matter under another law. The list is empty if no order is available.
GIVEN `what conduct is it about` IS A `Conduct complained of` @desc Which kind of conduct the application is about
      `am I an employee or prospective employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were or are an employee, or a prospective employee, of the person
      `was it about a position I applied for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was about a position you applied for (rather than a job you held)
      `has a court made a s 111 order for the same conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court, on convicting the person, has already ordered compensation or reinstatement for the same conduct
      `have I received compensation under another law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received compensation for the matter under another Commonwealth or State law
GIVETH A LIST OF `Order`
`What orders can the commission make for me` MEANS
    `s 112(3) -- the orders the commission may make for me` `what conduct is it about` `am I an employee or prospective employee` `was it about a position I applied for` `the other proceedings`
    WHERE
        `the other proceedings` MEANS
            `Other proceedings` WITH
                `a court made a s 111 order for the same conduct`        IS `has a court made a s 111 order for the same conduct`
                `another proceeding of mine on the same matter is pending` IS FALSE
                `I received compensation for the matter under another law` IS `have I received compensation under another law`
                `I failed in another proceeding on the same matter`        IS FALSE
                `that proceeding was about workers' compensation`          IS FALSE
                -- the second, fourth and fifth fields are read only by the s 115 bar: fixed placeholders here.

@export If the person who discriminated against me is convicted, what can the court order for me (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 111, 114(2))? On a conviction or finding of guilt for discriminatory conduct (s 104) or procuring it (s 107), the court may, besides the penalty, order compensation paid to you within a stated period and, if you were or are an employee or prospective employee, reinstatement, re-employment or employment in the position applied for. Not if the commission has already made a s 112 order for the same conduct.
GIVEN `have they been convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person has been convicted or found guilty of an offence against s 104 or s 107
      `am I an employee or prospective employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were or are an employee, or a prospective employee, of the person
      `was it about a position I applied for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was about a position you applied for
      `has the commission made a s 112 order for the same conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Industrial Relations Commission has already made an order under s 112 about the same conduct
GIVETH A LIST OF `Order`
`What can the court order for me on a conviction` MEANS
    `s 111 -- the orders the court may make for me` `have they been convicted` `am I an employee or prospective employee` `was it about a position I applied for` `has the commission made a s 112 order for the same conduct`

§§ `Prosecution of the person responsible`
-- Situation: the worker (or the family of a worker who died) wants the person responsible prosecuted.

@export What is the most the person responsible can be fined or imprisoned for (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 31-34, 34B-34D, 104-109)? Category 1 (reckless or negligent exposure to a risk of death or serious injury or illness): 3,000 penalty units or 5 years for an individual, 6,000 units or 5 years for a business owner or officer, 30,000 units for a body corporate. Category 2: 1,500 / 3,000 / 15,000 units. Category 3: 500 / 1,000 / 5,000 units. Industrial manslaughter: 20 years for an individual, 100,000 units for a body corporate. Discriminatory, coercive or misleading conduct: 1,000 units. A volunteer commits no category offence (except for a worker's or other person's own duty) and no industrial manslaughter. The answer is in penalty units (their dollar value is set under the Penalties and Sentences Act 1992) and years; 0 means none.
GIVEN `which offence` IS AN `Offence` @desc Which offence the person responsible would be charged with
      `who is the offender` IS AN `Offender` @desc Whether the offender is an individual acting otherwise than as a business owner or officer, an individual acting as a business owner (person conducting a business or undertaking) or officer, or a body corporate
      `is the offender a volunteer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offender is a volunteer
      `is the duty their own duty as a worker or other person at the workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For category 1-3: whether the duty broken is a worker's duty (s 28) or the duty of another person at the workplace (s 29)
GIVETH A `Penalty answer`
`What is the most the person responsible can face` MEANS
    `ss 31-34D, 104-109 -- the maximum penalty` `which offence` `who is the offender` `is the offender a volunteer` `is the duty their own duty as a worker or other person at the workplace`

@export Can I ask the WHS prosecutor in writing to bring a prosecution on this day (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 231(1)-(1B))? If no prosecution has been brought and you reasonably consider that what happened is a category 1 or 2 offence, you may ask from 6 months to 18 months after it happened; for industrial manslaughter, any time from 6 months after; if a coronial report or inquest shows a category 1 or 2 offence, within 6 months after the report was made or the inquest ended.
GIVEN `why do I consider an offence was committed` IS A `Why I consider an offence was committed` @desc The ground for your request (your belief must be a reasonable one)
      `has a prosecution been brought` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a prosecution has already been brought for the act or omission
      `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the act or omission happened
      `the day of the coronial report or end of the inquest` IS A DATE @desc The day the coronial report was made or the inquiry or inquest ended (any date if none)
      `the day of my request` IS A DATE @desc The day you would make the written request
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask for a prosecution on this day` MEANS
    `s 231(1B) -- I may request a prosecution` `why do I consider an offence was committed` `has a prosecution been brought` `the day it happened` `the day of the coronial report or end of the inquest` `the day of my request`

@export By when must the WHS prosecutor answer my request for a prosecution (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 231(2))? Within 3 months after receiving it, the WHS prosecutor must tell you in writing whether the investigation is complete and, if it is, whether a prosecution has been or will be brought or why not. If it is not complete, the regulator must update you in writing at least every 3 months until it is (s 231(2A)).
GIVEN `the day my request was received` IS A DATE @desc The day the WHS prosecutor received your written request
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the WHS prosecutor answer me` MEANS
    `s 231(2) -- the WHS prosecutor must advise me by` `the day my request was received`

@export Was (or would be) the WHS prosecutor's written answer to my request on this day in time (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 231(2))? In time up to and including the day 3 months after the request was received. If you have had no answer, give today's date: "no" means the time has run out.
GIVEN `the day my request was received` IS A DATE @desc The day the WHS prosecutor received your written request
      `the day of the answer, or today` IS A DATE @desc The day the written answer was given, or today's date if none has been given
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was the WHS prosecutor's answer in time` MEANS
    `yes or no` (`advise the applicant in writing` `by` `the WHS prosecutor` `on` `the day of the answer, or today` `is in time for` (`s 231(2) -- the WHS prosecutor must advise me by` `the day my request was received`))

@export By when must the WHS prosecutor refer my matter to the Director of Public Prosecutions (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 231(3))? If the WHS prosecutor tells you a prosecution for a category 1 or 2 offence or industrial manslaughter will not be brought, you may ask in writing for the matter to be referred to the DPP, and it must be referred within 1 month of your request.
GIVEN `the day of my request to refer` IS A DATE @desc The day you made your written request that the matter be referred to the DPP
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must my matter be referred to the DPP` MEANS
    `s 231(3)(b) -- the matter must be referred to the DPP by` `the day of my request to refer`

@export By when must the Director of Public Prosecutions advise on my matter (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 231(4), (5))? Within 1 month (read as 1 month after the referral), in writing to the WHS prosecutor, who must give you a copy; if the WHS prosecutor does not follow advice to prosecute, you must be given written reasons (s 231(6)).
GIVEN `the day of the referral` IS A DATE @desc The day the WHS prosecutor referred the matter to the DPP
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the DPP advise on my matter` MEANS
    `s 231(4) -- the DPP must advise by` `the day of the referral`

@export Until when can the person responsible be prosecuted (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 232)? Within the latest of: 2 years after the offence first came to the WHS prosecutor's notice; 1 year after a coronial report or inquest that showed an offence; and, if a WHS undertaking was given, 6 months after it was contravened, its contravention came to the regulator's notice, or its withdrawal was agreed. Industrial manslaughter has no time limit. A category 1 offence may be prosecuted later if a court accepts fresh evidence that could not reasonably have been found in time.
GIVEN `which offence` IS AN `Offence` @desc Which offence the person responsible would be charged with
      `the day it came to the WHS prosecutor's notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the offence first came to the notice of the WHS prosecutor
      `was there a coronial finding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appeared from a coronial report, inquiry or inquest that an offence against the Act had been committed
      `the day of the coronial report or end of the inquest` IS A DATE @desc The day the coronial report was made or the inquiry or inquest ended (any date if none)
      `was a WHS undertaking contravened or withdrawn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a WHS undertaking was given for the offence and was then contravened, or its contravention came to the regulator's notice, or the regulator agreed to its withdrawal
      `the day of that event` IS A DATE @desc The day of that contravention, notice or agreement (any date if none)
      `has a court accepted fresh evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a category 1 offence: whether a court is satisfied that fresh evidence has been discovered that could not reasonably have been discovered within the period
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which proceedings would be taken (used only for the fresh-evidence rule)
GIVETH A `Prosecution time limit`
`Until when can the person responsible be prosecuted` MEANS
    `s 232 -- the time to prosecute` `which offence` `the day it came to the WHS prosecutor's notice` `was there a coronial finding` `the day of the coronial report or end of the inquest` `was a WHS undertaking contravened or withdrawn` `the day of that event` `has a court accepted fresh evidence` `the day I am asking about`

§§ `Reviewing an inspector's or the regulator's decision`
-- Situation: an inspector or the regulator has made a decision (a notice) that affects the worker.

@export Can I apply for review of this decision (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 223, schedule 2A)? A worker whose interests are affected may seek review of decisions on provisional improvement notices, improvement notices and extensions of time to comply, prohibition notices, non-disturbance notices, subsequent notices and the regulator's variation or cancellation of a notice; so may the person the notice was issued to. Only the person entitled to a thing may seek review of its forfeiture or of a decision on returning seized things. For decisions prescribed by regulation, the regulation says who.
GIVEN `which decision` IS A `Decision` @desc Which decision you want reviewed
      `are my interests as a worker affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your interests as a worker are affected by the decision
      `was the notice issued to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the notice was issued to you
      `am I the person entitled to the thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a forfeiture or seized thing: whether you are the person from whom it was seized and entitled to possess it, or its owner
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply for review of this decision` MEANS
    `schedule 2A -- I am an eligible person` `which decision` `are my interests as a worker affected` `was the notice issued to me` `am I the person entitled to the thing`

@export What is my first step to have this decision reviewed, and by when (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 224, 229, 229B)? For a decision of an inspector (not the regulator or its delegate), apply to the regulator for internal review within 14 days after it came to your notice -- for an improvement notice, within the notice's compliance period if that is shorter -- or the longer period the regulator allows. For a decision of the regulator or its delegate, apply to the Industrial Relations Commission for external review within 30 days after it came to your notice (the commission may extend this).
GIVEN `which decision` IS A `Decision` @desc Which decision you want reviewed
      `was it made by the regulator or a delegate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made by the regulator or the regulator's delegate (rather than an inspector)
      `the day it came to my notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision first came to your notice
      `the compliance period in days` IS A NUMBER @desc For an improvement notice: the number of days the notice gives for compliance (any number otherwise)
      `the longer period the regulator allowed in days` IS A NUMBER @desc If the regulator has allowed a longer period for internal review, its length in days after the decision came to your notice (0 if none)
GIVETH A `Review step`
`What is my first review step and by when` MEANS
    `s 224 -- my first review step` `which decision` `was it made by the regulator or a delegate` `the day it came to my notice` `the compliance period in days` `the longer period the regulator allowed in days`

@export Is my application for review in time on this day (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 224, 229B)? In time up to and including the last day of the first review step (internal review, or external review by the commission for the regulator's own decisions).
GIVEN `which decision` IS A `Decision` @desc Which decision you want reviewed
      `was it made by the regulator or a delegate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made by the regulator or the regulator's delegate
      `the day it came to my notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision first came to your notice
      `the compliance period in days` IS A NUMBER @desc For an improvement notice: the number of days the notice gives for compliance (any number otherwise)
      `the longer period the regulator allowed in days` IS A NUMBER @desc If the regulator has allowed a longer period for internal review, its length in days (0 if none)
      `the day I would apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you would make the application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my review application in time on this day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 224 -- my first review step` `which decision` `was it made by the regulator or a delegate` `the day it came to my notice` `the compliance period in days` `the longer period the regulator allowed in days`
    WHEN `apply for internal review by` `the last day` THEN `in time for` `the last day`
    WHEN `no internal review: apply to the commission for external review by` `the last day` THEN `in time for` `the last day`
    -- NOTHING-kind: points outside the sources (a regulation and the QCAT Act).
    OTHERWISE NOTHING
    WHERE
        `in time for` `the last day` MEANS `yes or no` (`apply for review` `by` `the applicant` `on` `the day I would apply` `is in time for` `the last day`)

@export By when must I apply to the Industrial Relations Commission for external review of the decision on my internal review (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) ss 229, 229B)? Within 30 days after the day the decision first came to your notice; the commission may extend the time.
GIVEN `the day the decision came to my notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the internal review decision (or the regulator's decision) first came to your notice
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply to the commission for external review` MEANS
    `s 229B(2) -- the last day to file` `the day the decision came to my notice`

@export If the internal reviewer does not decide, when is the decision taken to be confirmed (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 226)? The reviewer must decide within 14 days after receiving your application; the 14 days stop running while you are providing further information the reviewer asked for. If the decision is not varied or set aside within that time it is taken to be confirmed, and you can then seek external review.
GIVEN `the day my application was received` IS A DATE @desc The day the regulator received your application for internal review
      `days paused while I provided information` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days between the reviewer's request for further information and your providing it (0 if none)
GIVETH A DATE
`When is my internal review decision taken to be confirmed` MEANS
    `s 226(6) -- the day my decision is taken to be confirmed` `the day my application was received` `days paused while I provided information`

@export Does my application for internal review put the decision on hold (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 228)? Yes, except for a prohibition notice or a non-disturbance notice: for those the reviewer may stay it, must decide a stay application within 1 working day, and is taken to have granted the stay if they do not.
GIVEN `which decision` IS A `Decision` @desc Which decision you applied to have reviewed
      `what happened to a stay` IS A `Stay request` @desc For a prohibition or non-disturbance notice: whether you applied for a stay, and what the reviewer did
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my internal review application stay the decision` MEANS
    `s 228 -- my internal review application stays the decision` `which decision` `what happened to a stay`

§§ `My other rights`

@export Does the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) give me a right to sue for a breach of it (s 267)? Only for discriminatory or coercive conduct (Part 6, through the Industrial Relations Commission). Otherwise the Act gives no civil right of action for a breach, and it does not take away or limit any right you have under the general law (negligence) or the workers' compensation scheme.
GIVEN `what does my claim rest on` IS A `What my claim rests on` @desc What the claim rests on
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the Act give me a right to sue` MEANS
    `s 267 -- the Act gives me a civil right of action` `what does my claim rest on`

@export Is this term of my contract or agreement void (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 272)? A term that purports to exclude, limit or modify the operation of the Act or any duty owed under it, or to transfer a duty under it to another person, is void.
GIVEN `does it exclude, limit or modify the Act or a duty under it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term purports to exclude, limit or modify the operation of the Act or a duty owed under it (for example, that you accept all safety risks)
      `does it transfer a duty to someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term purports to transfer a duty owed under the Act to another person
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this term of my contract void` MEANS
    `s 272 -- the term is void` `does it exclude, limit or modify the Act or a duty under it` `does it transfer a duty to someone else`

@export Can the business charge me for this safety item or measure (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 273)? No: a person conducting a business or undertaking must not impose, or permit, a levy or charge on a worker for anything done or provided in relation to work health and safety, such as protective equipment or training (maximum penalty 50 penalty units).
GIVEN `am I a worker of the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker of the business (see the worker question)
      `is the charge for something done or provided for work health and safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the levy or charge is for anything done or provided in relation to work health and safety
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the business charge me for this` MEANS
    `s 273 -- the business may charge me` (`yes or no` `am I a worker of the business`) `is the charge for something done or provided for work health and safety`

@export Can I claim compensation from the State for loss caused by an inspector's entry or search (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Qld) s 184)? You may claim if you incurred loss or expense because of the exercise, or purported exercise, of an inspector's powers relating to entry (Part 9 Division 3), in a court or in a prosecution against you; the court may order it only if satisfied that it is just.
GIVEN `did I incur loss or expense` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you incurred loss or expense
      `was it because of an inspector's entry powers` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was because of the exercise or purported exercise of an inspector's powers of entry, search, seizure or the like under Part 9 Division 3
      `is the court satisfied it is just` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied it is just to order compensation in your case (a finding for the court)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get compensation for an inspector's entry` MEANS
    `s 184(3) -- the court may order compensation` (`s 184(1) -- I may claim compensation from the State` `did I incur loss or expense` `was it because of an inspector's entry powers`) `is the court satisfied it is just`
